1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,432
In 1937,

2
00:00:00,432 --> 00:00:02,971
a twenty-six-year-old British economist wrote a

3
00:00:02,971 --> 00:00:04,916
paper that quietly explains why tiny

4
00:00:04,916 --> 00:00:08,374
one-to-three-person software businesses now rival hundred-person

5
00:00:08,374 --> 00:00:09,400
agencies in profit.

6
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:10,188
In Economica,

7
00:00:10,188 --> 00:00:12,613
future Nobel laureate Ronald Coase asked

8
00:00:12,613 --> 00:00:14,795
why large corporations exist at all.

9
00:00:14,795 --> 00:00:16,795
His answer was transaction costs:

10
00:00:16,795 --> 00:00:18,371
searching for specialists,

11
00:00:18,371 --> 00:00:19,705
negotiating contracts,

12
00:00:19,705 --> 00:00:22,190
and policing quality across open markets.

13
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:23,201
For eighty years,

14
00:00:23,201 --> 00:00:26,056
high transaction costs forced ambitious founders

15
00:00:26,056 --> 00:00:27,721
to hire big internal staffs.

16
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:28,078
Today,

17
00:00:28,078 --> 00:00:31,170
many entrepreneurs still chase headcount for status,

18
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:33,430
recreating slow corporate bureaucracy.

19
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:34,112
Modern APIs,

20
00:00:34,112 --> 00:00:34,907
cloud billing,

21
00:00:34,907 --> 00:00:37,634
and AI workflows collapse coordination friction.

22
00:00:37,634 --> 00:00:39,565
Build Coasean micro-firms instead:

23
00:00:39,565 --> 00:00:41,667
keep core ownership tiny and automate

24
00:00:41,667 --> 00:00:44,110
repeatable production before hiring anyone.

25
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:46,624
Measure your business by free cashflow

26
00:00:46,624 --> 00:00:48,940
and calm calendar hours per person,

27
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:51,190
never by crowded office headcount.
